rules.content_en:
#### Rule Overview
When using the Symfony Console component, if the defined command parameters are not correctly passed during invocation, it will result in an error. This rule aims to ensure that all defined command parameters are correctly set during invocation.

##### Rule Significance
Ensuring that the parameters of Symfony Console commands can be properly processed to avoid runtime errors and enhance code robustness. This detection helps developers identify and fix potential errors during the coding phase, preventing issues in the production environment.

##### Error Example
```php
// Error code example:
use Symfony\Component\Console\Command\Command;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Input\InputInterface;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Output\OutputInterface;

class ExampleCommand extends Command {
    protected function configure() {
        $this->setName('example:run')
             ->addArgument('name', InputArgument::REQUIRED, 'The name of the user.');
    }

    protected function execute(InputInterface $input, OutputInterface $output) {
        $name = $input->getArgument('username'); // Parameter not found
        $output->writeln('Hello ' . $name);
    }
}
```

##### Correct Example
```php
// Correct code example:
use Symfony\Component\Console\Command\Command;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Input\InputInterface;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Output\OutputInterface;

class ExampleCommand extends Command {
    protected function configure() {
        $this->setName('example:run')
             ->addArgument('name', InputArgument::REQUIRED, 'The name of the user.');
    }

    protected function execute(InputInterface $input, OutputInterface $output) {
        $name = $input->getArgument('name'); // Using the correct parameter name
        $output->writeln('Hello ' . $name);
    }
}
```

